---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { CURRENT_VERSION } from '../../lib/versions.ts';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const u = (r: string) => base.replace(/\/$/, '') + r;
---

<Layout
  title="About the Playbook"
  description="What the Adaptive AI Systems Playbook is, how its rules earn their place, and how to read it."
  section="about"
>
  <header class="doc-head">
    <div class="doc-eyebrow">
      <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} />
    </div>
    <h1>About this playbook</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The <a href={u('/playbook/')}>Adaptive AI Systems Playbook</a> answers one question, over and
      over, at every stage of a project: <em>what should I do next, and what evidence would justify
      it?</em> It covers the whole arc — deciding what to build, building something that can measure
      it, running experiments whose answers you can act on, choosing models and runtimes, optimizing,
      deploying, and knowing when to stop.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is written to be project-independent. Nothing in it assumes a particular company, domain,
      budget, or stack. This site renders version {CURRENT_VERSION}.
    </p>

    <h2>How a rule earns its place</h2>
    <p>
      Every claim in the book carries a label saying how much weight it can bear. This matters more
      than it sounds, because the honest answer is often "less than you'd like":
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>consensus</strong> — widely agreed across independent practitioners and vendors.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>strong evidence</strong> — supported by published results that were checked, with the
        source recorded in the <a href={u('/references/sources/')}>ledger</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>inference</strong> — reasoned from first principles and argued in place. Useful, but
        not measured. The book says so rather than dressing it up.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <a href={u('/references/sources/')}>source ledger</a> lists every citation with a verdict —
      FOLLOW, ADAPT, REFERENCE, or DEPRECATED — and the date it was last checked. Guidance that has
      aged badly is marked, not quietly deleted.
    </p>

    <h2>What the scenarios are and aren't</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={u('/cases/')}>scenarios</a> are invented. Every project, number, and name in them
      is fictional, and they are labelled that way on every page. Each one takes a rule the book
      argues for, shows a team ignoring it for entirely reasonable-sounding motives, and follows what
      that cost.
    </p>
    <p>
      They illustrate rules. They are never the evidence for them. Where the book claims something is
      established, the support is an external source in the ledger — never a scenario.
    </p>

    <h2>How to read it</h2>
    <p>
      Start with the <a href={u('/playbook/quickstart/')}>Quickstart</a>, which routes you by what
      kind of project you have and what is at stake. The <a href={u('/learn/')}>lifecycle map</a>
      shows how the chapters connect if you'd rather see the shape first. The
      <a href={u('/playbook/glossary/')}>glossary</a> defines every term the book uses, and those
      definitions appear on hover throughout the site — so you should never need to leave a page to
      understand it.
    </p>
    <p>
      You do not have to adopt the whole thing. Chapters are written to be useful on their own, and
      the <a href={u('/playbook/00-principles-and-scope/')}>rigor dial</a> in chapter 00 exists
      precisely so that a weekend project and a regulated deployment can follow the same book without
      pretending to be each other.
    </p>
  </div>

  <footer class="site-footer">
    Playbook v{CURRENT_VERSION} · <a href={u('/changelog/')}>Changelog</a>
  </footer>
</Layout>
